    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Stackable vs Shelf</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#specs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Specs That Matter</a>
          <a href="#jerky" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Dehydrating Jerky Safely</a>
          <a href="#fruit-veg" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Fruit, Vegetables and Herbs</a>
          <a href="#running-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Power, Noise and Space</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Cleaning and Care</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best food dehydrator is the one whose airflow and capacity match your batch. Shelf-style stainless units with a large fan dry evenly and suit jerky and big harvests; stackable round units are cheaper and expandable but often need trays rotated. Decide how much you dry at once, then buy the design that handles it without babysitting.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Food dehydrators look simple: a heater, a fan and some trays in a box. The differences that decide whether you will enjoy using one are in the details, and most of them do not appear in a product photo. Where is the fan? Does air blow across every tray evenly, or does the tray nearest the heater dry first? Can you set a temperature high enough for jerky? Is there a timer, so you can walk away? How much can you dry at once, and how much counter space does it take?
          </p>

          <p>
            We built this list from manufacturer specifications and product pages. Nesco publishes wattage, temperature ranges, timer limits and tray counts on its product pages, which we quote directly. Cosori's store lists tray counts, materials and prices, which we quote, but at the time of writing we could not confirm wattage, temperature range or timer limits from Cosori's pages, so we say so on each card rather than filling in numbers from memory. We did not run our own drying trials and we do not publish drying-time scores.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also left out models we could not verify. Excalibur, for example, is a well-known shelf dehydrator brand with a loyal following, but we could not confirm current model specifications from the manufacturer while writing this, so it does not appear as a pick. If you are considering one, compare its published wattage, temperature range and timer against the criteria below.
          </p>

          <p>
            The result is a short list that covers the main buying decisions: a mid-sized stainless shelf unit for most kitchens, a dual-zone machine for big harvests, compact and large Cosori shelf units, a premium option, and an expandable stackable for anyone starting on a budget.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Stackable vs Shelf Dehydrators
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stackable dehydrators are the round or square towers where trays sit on top of each other and the heater and fan sit at the top or the bottom. They are usually the cheapest option, they take little counter space, and many can be expanded by adding trays. Nesco's Snackmaster Pro is the classic example, listed as expanding from 5 to 12 trays. The trade-off is airflow: air has to travel through the stack, so trays closest to the fan may dry faster, and you may need to rotate them during long runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shelf dehydrators, sometimes called box or cabinet dehydrators, look like a small oven. Trays slide in from the front, and the fan usually blows air across or through the trays from the back or the center. Nesco describes its stainless units as having a large, centrally located fan for even heat distribution. The advantages are more even drying, easier loading and the ability to remove a single tray to check progress without disturbing the others. The trade-offs are a bigger, fixed footprint and a higher price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fan position matters</h3>

          <p>
            In a stackable, a top-mounted fan has a real practical advantage: liquid from juicy fruit or marinated meat cannot drip into the heater. Nesco makes exactly this point for the Snackmaster Pro. Bottom-mounted fans work, but drips can reach the heating element, which makes cleanup harder and can smoke. In a shelf unit, a rear or central fan pushing air across the trays generally gives the most even results, which is why shelf units are favored for big batches of jerky.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most households that dry regularly, a shelf unit is worth the extra money. For occasional snacks, trying the hobby out or small kitchens, a good stackable is the sensible start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            These six were chosen from manufacturer specifications and product listings, not lab testing. Nesco publishes wattage, temperature range and timer details on its product pages, which we quote directly; for Cosori we quote the tray count, material and price shown on Cosori's own store and leave out figures we could not confirm. Prices shown are the maker's listed prices at the time of writing and change often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
